My advice at this point is to go through the process of compiling
and upgrading the kernel yourself.  RedHat includes support for all
kinds of hardware that you don't have, maybe something is getting
in the way.

Try to compile the kernel with no modules at all, answer Y or N
but not M.  Going through the process is time consuming but I've
always preferred to do it that way.  Here's my steps for the
first time you do it
:

get new kernel source from ftp.kernel.org:

cd /usr/src
mkdir new
cd new
ncftpget ftp://ftp.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v2.2/linux-2.2.15.tar.bz2

unpack kernel:

tar xvf linux-2.2.15.tar.bz2 --use-compress-program bzip2
mv linux ../linux-2.2.15
cd ..
rm linux
ln -s linux-2.2.15 linux

build kernel, the first step takes 20-75 minutes depending on how much reading you do:

make config
make dep
make boot	# note, if you're used to Intel, this step is different, don't try to make zImage
make modules		# note, shouldn't do anything if you didn't have any modules
make modules-install

copy things where they need to go:

cp System.map /boot/System.map-2.2.15
cp arch/alpha/boot/vmlinux.gz /boot/vmlinux-2.2.15.gz
cd /boot
ls -l System.map vmlinuz
rm System.map vmlinuz		# check to make sure these are symbolic links first!!
ln -s System.map-2.2.15 System.map
ln -s vmlinux-2.2.15.gz vmlinuz

reboot.  Make sure to tell your bootloader that the kernel is called /boot/vmlinuz
On my AlphaBios/milo boxes this is:

boot sda2:/boot/vmlinuz root=/dev/sda2

That's what I do when I don't have an /usr/src/linux/.config file made up
from a previous run.  In that case, instead of make config, copy your old
.config file from your old kernel source directory and either say 
'make oldconfig' if you are changing kernel versions and don't want anything
different in the new kernel or you can say 'make config' and have the option
of changing your responses.  It is sometimes faster to just edit the .config
file and 'make oldconfig', but I don't recommend it since 'make' changes
what options are available depending on what your previous responses are.

If having the option 'CONFIG_DEC_ELCP=y' doesn't work, try 'CONFIG_DE4X5=y'
instead.

Best of luck,

Tony Hammitt


P.S. I knocked this off mostly from memory, if anyone else has an opinion
about what to do in this case, feel free to post a critique.


