Start your first partition on Cylinder "2", that will leave enough space for
aboot to be written without running into the first partition.

If you know what partition the "/boot" or "/" is you can use this or a
variation depending what the disk name is in "srm show dev"

boot dka0 -fi 1/vmlinux.gz -fl "root=/dev/sda2"
1/vmlinux.gz  (assumes /boot is on the first partition and the kernel is
named vmlinux.gz

"root=/dev/sda2" (assumes root is on the second partition)

OR

boot dka0 -fi 2/vmlinux.gz -fl "root=/dev/sda2"
assumes the kernel and root are on the same partition.

----
Finally if its like RH6.2 you can use

boot dka0 -fl 0
Since they setup aboot.conf in /etc which aboot can find and read predefined
startup flags set by RedHat or a user.

JJ
.



