In article <3922EDF2.32E642BA@kdc.no>, Idar Tollefsen  <idar@kdc.no> wrote:
>When booting and starting inetd it displays the following message:
>"neighbour talbe overflow"
>several times. If I do a dmesg, this message is listed under the entry 
>for "adding swap", but I still think this is inetd's fault and that
>it's just been logged after the adding of swap space.

No, it's a kernel message. Which means that a kernel bug was triggered
or at least something happened that shouldn't ever happen.

I was the same message last week - turns out that the kernel and a
lot of apps don't like it if you don't have a loopback interface
setup  (i.e. interface "lo" with IP address 127.0.0.1)

Mike.



