You may be able to compile some of the kernel source files
with ccc but there are lots of files that have gcc specific
assembler instructions, etc.

It would be a lot of effort compiling all of the possible
files with ccc, then the rest with gcc, and what you would
end up with may even boot.  But I wouldn't guarantee it.

More to the point, the kernel isn't really the best thing
to try massive optimizations with anyway.  Unless your
system is extremely heavily loaded, the kernel doesn't
really take up all that much processor time.  That and if
it's a really time critical piece of code, the chances are
that it's already in hand-optimized assembler.

What would be more interesting is trying to run something
like 'spike', the post-analyzing optimizer (see the news
at www.alphalinux.org) on the kernel.  It may be able to
get some further optimizations but you'd have to run it
using user-mode linux, probably.

I think getting the libc stuff and XFree4.0 compiled using 
ccc would be more likely to show benefits and would probably
be lots easier.

But don't let me discourage you =-]  Try getting the kernel
working and let us know how it comes out...

Regards,

Tony Hammitt



