Centuries ago, Nostradamus foresaw a time when Shannon & Angela Menkveld
would say:
>I am getting an Alpha board for free from my uncle.  The board is a 164UX,
>with a 21164/600.  I am brand-new to the alpha architechture, and I have
>some questions. Actually a whole bunch, but I'll limit them.
>
>    1. What are the limitaitons of the various Linux/Alpha distributions.
>I.E., 64-    bit compilation, processor/MB support, big differences between
>linux for x86     and alpha.

There's a fair bit of code that doesn't work as well on Alpha due
to 32-bit-isms.  Less than there used to be, and every added 64 bit
architecture that gets added helps.

>    2. The board has one 64-bit PCI slot, and since the SCSI controller is
>on the     board, I figured that the best use of this slot would be for the
>video card.          However, I can't seem to find any video cards which go
>into a 64-bit PCI         slot.  I have spent many fruitless hours searcing
>the net for this, and I thought     this newsgroup could help me.

That's a good question; I don't think you have any video cards to choose
from at present.  There may not be anything really good to use the slot
for.

>    3. If there are no good 64-bit video cards available, can anyone
>recommend     a fast 32-bit PCI video card which is known to work well in
>this board.  I         want this system to be as fast as it can be, and I
>don't want to bottleneck the     CPU with a slow video card.

Matrox Millennium is well-supported; Permedia cards as well; S3 Virge
is what i have in my PC164 machine.  Many of the chipsets supported on
IA-32 do _NOT_ work on Alpha; choose carefully...

>    4. This board was sold as an NT 4.0 board.  I have never heard of a
>motherboard being sold for a specific OS, what problems would this cause if
>I want to use this board in a Linux box?

Ah.  At the time of sale, Digital was selling products mostly to three
markets:
a) Windows NT
b) VMS
c) Digital UNIX

Support of VMS and DU required special microcode and such; pretty much
_all_ the PCI-bus-based hardware has worked with Linux.  It was Digital's
own OSes that support was spotty for.  (Which sounds strange, but true...)

>I have searched the LDP page, all of the HOWTO's whioch came with my x86
>Linux distribution, and 4 books, and I can't seem to answer these questions.
>Any help would be greatly appreciated.

See the URL below for links to major Linux/Alpha sites.  You should be
looking to Alpha-specific sites for information.
