www.suse.com

>     2. The board has one 64-bit PCI slot, and since the SCSI
controller is
> on the     board, I figured that the best use of this slot would be
for the
> video card.          However, I can't seem to find any video cards
which go
> into a 64-bit PCI         slot.  I have spent many fruitless hours
searcing
> the net for this, and I thought     this newsgroup could help me.

I can't think of any 64-bit video cards supported by Linux off the top
of my head, but you can stick a 32-bit PCI card in a 64-bit slot. In
fact for *this* board you will have to do this in order for milo to work
with a PCI video card.

>     3. If there are no good 64-bit video cards available, can anyone
> recommend     a fast 32-bit PCI video card which is known to work well
in
> this board.  I         want this system to be as fast as it can be,
and I
> don't want to bottleneck the     CPU with a slow video card.

The PCI Matrox Millennium cards work great.

>     4. This board was sold as an NT 4.0 board.  I have never heard of
a
> motherboard being sold for a specific OS, what problems would this
cause if
> I want to use this board in a Linux box?

Alpha motherboards that were made for NT came with ARC or AlphaBIOS.
Tru64, OpenVMS, and *BSD have to have the SRM BIOS. Most boards can be
upgraded to SRM, although the 164UX cannot. It was originally developed
by Deskstation and uses their own BIOS called ARCS BIOS (note the "S").
With the help of linload and milo, Linux runs great on these boards.

