Shannon & Angela Menkveld <shannon.menkveld@gte.net> wrote:

>     2. The board has one 64-bit PCI slot, and since the SCSI controller is
> on the     board, I figured that the best use of this slot would be for the
> video card.

In fact, the firmware requires a video card to be present in this
particular slot or it won't start up.

>     3. If there are no good 64-bit video cards available, can anyone
> recommend     a fast 32-bit PCI video card which is known to work well in
> this board.

With the market changing monthly, I really have no idea what is
considered fast these days. S3 and Matrox cards generally seem to
work fine in alphas. I have an ATI Xpert@Work.

>     4. This board was sold as an NT 4.0 board.  I have never heard of a
> motherboard being sold for a specific OS, what problems would this cause if
> I want to use this board in a Linux box?

None in particular. The firmware can only boot from a FAT partition.

The boot sequence is somewhat convulated:
- firmware loads ldmilo.exe from FAT partition
- ldmilo.exe loads MILO from same partition
- MILO loads Linux kernel from ext2fs root partition

You can't run any other alpha operating systems (OpenVMS, Tru64,
*BSD) on that board, since they all require the SRM console firmware
which isn't available.

