eVelHakUr (george_m_kent@yahoo.com) wrote:

: Keep in mind if your hard drive is over 8 gig you will not be able to load
: LILO,

This is Alpha so you will be able to load LILO in any case but the
current versions of LILO are supposed to be be good up to 2 TB of disk
(no, I did not test it :-).

OTOH various versions of MILO and aboot on alpha (maybe all, I do not
know) will not load kernels with offsets bigger than 2 GB into
a __partition__ so it is a good idea to have a partition where you
keep your boot images smaller than that.

There is another "gotcha" to keep in mind.  Milos floating around are
(mostly?) based on 2.0 kernel code and therefore do not know anything
about ext2 file systems with features like 'sparse_superblocks' and
block sizes other than 1024 hence will be not able to read any files
from these.  Current versions of e2fsprogs will use 'sparse_superblock'
and 4096 sized blocks - if a file system is "big enough" - by default;
but can be told, of course, not to do that.  Filesystem features can be
modfied with tune2fs but to change a block size you will have to
reformat.

I do not know about aboot-0.7 but older versions of aboot will
have the same limitations (a file systems reader code in aboot
is quite simplified).

Red Hat installer is "aware" about the issue.  If you are laying
out file systems yourself, or if you are using some other installers,
you better watch your steps.

  Michal



