Jack Edwards (jackedwa@mediaone.net) wrote:
: Question:
: Do you have a line like 'alias eth0 de4x5' in your 'modules.conf'?

: Answer:
: My system does not have a modules.conf.  It has the file conf.modules and it
: does contain this line.

The same thing for purposes of this discussion (which you can learn
from documenation).  Current versions of 'modutils' will raise ruckus
about that but this is irrelevant for the moment.


: Question:
: Any other options?

: Answer:
: The file contains the following two lines:
: Alias eth0 de4x5
: Alias parport_lowlevel parport_pc

Translation - "no other options".

: Question:
: Is module really inserted (what 'lsmod' has to say)?

: Answer:
: It displays a header line but nothing else.

Well, then find out what is wrong here.  If you do not have a driver
loaded then you cannot expect your connection to work.

: The first time I type modprobe -v de4x5 it displayed the following message:

: Modeprobe: can't open dependencies file
: /lib/modules/2.2.5-14BOOT/modules.dep (No such file or directory)

Bingo!  We have a winner!  You are booting not that kernel for which
have modules - if you installed any.  Instead of using that one for an
installation ("BOOT") use the one which come for that purpose on your
CD.  If something went wrong during installation then grab kernel, and
its modules, from rpm archives in your distribution.  Use rpm to
install.

A kernel version string (you will see it when you type 'uname -r')
and a name of a directory /lib/modules/<that_one> have to agree.
That is about everything for now.

  Michal



