  Hi, Mike. Still masturbating that ego, I see.
  
  I'll concede that I can't find any FAQ's out there anymore that recommend
that linux users use 2* the physical memory. However, the rule of thumb that was
recommended in the linux FAQ when I first started using linux was 2* the 
physical memory. FreeBSD recommends this still. It's a good rule because even 
though you may have gargantuan amounts of physical memory, intensive 
calculations and big applications can eat this up very quickly. An example of 
this is some quantum chromodynamics code an acquaintence of mine was running. 
It consumed his close to 1 GB of memory and still used swap. In fact, quoting 
one of the FAQs for Debian/Alpha,

   With respect to the issue of swap partition size, there are many
   views. One rule of thumb which works well is to use as much swap as
   you have system memory, although there probably isn't much point in
   going over 64MB of swap for most users. It also shouldn't be smaller
   than 16MB, in most cases. Of course, there are exceptions to these
   rules. If you are trying to solve 10000 simultaneous equations on a
   machine with 256MB of memory, you may need a gigabyte (or more) of
   swap.

  I personally have 128M or ram in my box and 2* the physical memory for 
swap. I've seen goodly chunks of swap being used during large compiles.  
  In any event, under linux these days no one seems to have a solid answer.
So. Use 2*, don't use 2*, I couldn't care less. 

Neener.

