My time had been absorbed by other projects, but now I have redone the
test. Below are part of the results. I have modified the code a little:
Now I write the pattern once, but then read it twice. So I can see if
the data is corrupted when reading or writing. It turned out that data
is nearly always corrupted when reading (i.E. two succeeding reads find
mismatches at different possitions). Sometimes two read checks show
identical mismatches. This might be coincidence, or it might indicate
that sometimes data is also corrupted when writing. To me, the patterns
look more like single bit errors.

What really bothers me is that I only see the problem with the FF
pattern. There never is a SINGLE error with the other patterns (the
test ran much longer than the log file excerpt shown below).

I am also at a loss as to why I never see a single machine check, ECC
correctable or non-correctable error, etc. Those should happen often,
if the machine is really so flaky?

Guess I'll have no chance but to replace that ominous 74F623 chip and
hope that this helps?

