Reply-To: mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

I've been working for a couple of weeks getting my 9 node DS10 cluster
to boot RH 6.2 over the net and, well, generally just play nice as a 
Beowulf.  Most of the scripts were supplied by Puget Sound Data Systems
along with the hardware, but needed to be modified substantially to work
with 6.2 (instead of the 6.1 which shipped on the systems.) A new bootp.c
file provided, which allows the vmlinux.bootp kernel to be preconfigured
with a list of known nodes, and so boot them without os flags.  The 
multiple boot device capability of SRM is used to let the dhcpd server
control how remote nodes boot on power on - either over the net or from the 
local disk.

Hopefully this information will save somebody else a few weeks of work.
Pick it up at 

  http://seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu/pub/SOFTWARE/nodeinstall.tgz

and unpack it as /node-install on the master (boot) node.  Then read
INSTRUCTIONS in that directory for more information.

(If anybody figures out the /etc/rc.d/rc3.d changes needed to let a network 
node shutdown properly please send them to me!  In the meantime, there's 
the power switch on the front of the DS10s...)

Regards,

David Mathog
mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
Manager, sequence analysis facility, biology division, Caltech 



